th criteria as evaluation-time scoring rules applied to identical frozen model outputs from the BBH 23+4 task list, so the manipulation is solely at the scoring step; EM is the {0,1} exact-match indicator and MinED is the normalized Levenshtein-style alignment score in [0,1]. Because MinED is mechanically >= EM on any instance, any raw-mean comparison is rejected as uninformative and the design pivots to operational definitions A (gradedness/discrimination) and B (agreement with a quality proxy), each with a pre-registered pre-registered rule, paired statistical stack, and explicit effect-size thresholds; the negative control is the forced-EM rerun of the MinED code path, determinism is double-run EM, and per-task-type stratification (23 MC vs 4 free-form) is reported separately. No empirical numbers are produced; no installation or execution is attempted.\", \"assumptions\": [\"MinED equals a normalized Minimum Edit Distance alignment score, of the form 1 - edit_distance(predicted, gold) / max(len(predicted), len(gold)), with edit distance applied at a tokenization (character or BPE) that is to be matched to the paper; treated as a scoring-stage criterion only, not a decoding strategy.\", \"EM equals Exact Match on the final extracted answer, returning 1 if normalized predicted equals normalized gold and 0 otherwise; for MC tasks this is checked on the canonical option-letter comparison.\", \"BBH evaluation set equals the papers 23+4 task configuration (23 multiple-choice tasks + 4 free-form tasks), with both criteria applicable per task and per task-type stratification reported in the design.\", \"The manipulation is purely at the scoring step: same frozen LLM weights, same greedy decoding, same CoT few-shot prompt templates, same max-new-tokens cap, same RNG seed, same per-instance predicted-text strings; only the matching rule varies.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Required: which operational definition of outperforms the paper uses on the Alignment criterion - defin